t. I'd bundle into one Dashboard remediation epic, flagging (bundled) with rationale, OR split. Hmm.\n\nActually, think about boundary axes: same component, same team. Architectural sub-system: all frontend React UI. Work-product: code. So one epic for the code fixes covering HLR-1 and HLR-2 is the natural shape. Splitting by flow (four epics) would be priority-based? No — flows could be separate epics along... no, they're the same surface type, same team, same logical change (audit remediation). One epic.\n\nBut wait — is one epic too big? Four flows keyboard operability + focus indicators. The audit enumerates exact defects. Probably a couple weeks of work. Keep as one epic; it's one logical sweep. Alternatively split into two: HLR-1 (keyboard operability) and HLR-2 (focus indicators). They share UI surfaces (the same four flows), so bundling is defensible. I'll bundle into one Implementation epic with both HLRs at P1, flagging HLR-2 row as bundled with rationale (shared surfaces, same audit, same sweep).\n\n- HLR-3 (P2): VPAT + accessibility statement update, republished, with technical review → docs-authoring epic, component Documentation, blocked by the Dashboard implementation epic (Rule 11: docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics). Priority: docs-authoring exception — strategy frontmatter priority: Normal → P2. Good, matches HLR-3's P2.\n\nTeams: strategy doesn't give an Impacted Teams table. Dashboard is owned by... I need team names. The strategy doesn't specify teams. Common convention in this eval set: \"Dashboard\" team? Let me check architecture context files for team info. Let me read the Dashboard and Documentation architecture context files.\n\nAlso note missing sections: Affected Components is a list not a table (no change/owner), no Impacted Teams table, no dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria section, no NFRs, no technical approach. Health warnings: missing sections. No Staff Engineer Input section (template placeholder? not present at all — treat as absent).\n\nInvestigation epics: Open Questions: None. No conditional ADRs. No genuine unknowns. So no Investigation epics.\n\nDAG: E001 (Dashboard keyboard remediation, P1) --> E002 (docs-authoring, P2). critical_path_length=2. epic_count=2.\n\nRule 24: docs-authoring epic gets \"technically reviewed against implementation\" AC — HLR-3 already says \"with technical review.\"\n\nACs for E001 must be testable on its own deliverable: e.g., each of the four flows completable by keyboard alone with no focus traps (per audit defect list, all defects verified fixed), focus indicators consistent per audit criteria, regression tests. Something like \"all defects enumerated in the audit report for the four flows are resolved and verified by keyboard-only walkthrough.\"\n\nTeams: need owner team names. Check architecture-context Dashboard file.\n\nSignal scoring for E001 (Implementation):\n1. change_specificity: +1 (audit enumerates exact defects per flow)\n2. pattern_precedent: probably +1 or 0 — similar accessibility fixes exist in Dashboard? Unknown. Let me check the Dashboard architecture context. Probably +1 if PatternFly provides focus management conventions... safer 0 or +1. Let me read the context file first.\n3. adapter_pattern: 0 (N/A)\n4. existing_foundation: +1 (fixing existing flows, not greenfield)\n5. open_questions: +1 (none; audit enumerates defects)\n6. external_dependency: +1 (none) — wait, +1 condition is \"None\". Yes, no external deps → +1.\n7. human_process_gates: +1? None for the code epic. VPAT publication might involve review but that's the docs epic. For E001, no human approval → +1.\n8. repo_access: +1 (odh-dashboard repo, public)\n9. architecture_claims: check Dashboard context file. The strategy claims Dashboard has four core flows with keyboard issues — context file corroborates Dashboard component exists with those flows? Score based on file.\n\nFor E002 (docs-authoring, Implementation type with implementation_type=docs-aut